# A local alias of a local alias of the source root, written to by path in native L2 (an .lm2 file): the roots resolve
# at translation (`top` -> `shared` -> `node[0]`), no initializer runs, and every store is the unchecked store of the
# field's receiving context, so interp, native and forced answer the same. `top\Source\x := 9` replaces Source's int
# fragment; Holder's slot 0 addressed the former fragment of x and keeps it (x 1); `top\Source\p := @(top\Target\y)`
# gives the reference field p its target (the book, #frame-and-graph-assignment: "Replacing one cell's fragment does
# not implicitly replace the other cell's fragment"; #correspondence-stability: "Named receiving places use the
# ordinary admission contract"). Not a copy of path_write_native_level2: that row writes through `shared` itself.
(
    Holder:
        int: h 0
    Target:
        int: y 7
    Source:
        int: x 1
        @: p int 0
)
sub: main ()
    @: shared node[0]
    @: top shared
    top\Holder[0] := @(top\Source\x)
    top\Source\x := 9
    top\Source\p := @(top\Target\y)
main()
